title: The Leipzig Congress
subtitle: The Eighth Party Congress meets at the end of January.
new-page: true
tags: rf_event
view-if: congress_number = 8 and time >= 2
max-visits: 1
priority: 3
on-arrival: {!
var RF = window.RF;
RF.normalize(Q);
Q.mo_passed_news = '';
var d = RF.delegateShares(Q);
Q.leipzig_delegates = RF.delegateHemicycle(d, 150);
Q.leipzig_legend = RF.factionLegend(d);
Q.leipzig_districts = RF.districtsHtml(Q);
!}

= The Eighth Party Congress, Leipzig

Leipzig, the last days of January 1923. A fortnight ago French and Belgian troops marched into Essen to take the coal Germany owes in reparations. The Cuno government, a cabinet of shipowners and bankers with no party behind it, has answered with passive resistance and the printing press, and the mark is falling faster than at any time since the war. Prices change between morning and evening. In Dresden the Saxon government has just fallen, and Social Democrats there are asking aloud whether they could govern with Communist votes. In Munich, this same week, Hitler's National Socialists parade under their new standards.

The party that meets here is barely four years old. It has lived through the murder of its founders, a split, a merger with the left of the Independents, a failed rising and the expulsion of its former chairman. From the International's Fourth Congress in Moscow it has brought home two slogans, the united front and the workers' government, and no agreement on what they mean. The Zentrale's majority, led by <span class="m-brandler rf-person">Heinrich Brandler</span> and <span class="m-thalheimer rf-person">August Thalheimer</span>, reads them as a patient road through the factories, the unions and governments like Saxony's. The Berlin organisation of <span class="m-fischer rf-person">Ruth Fischer</span> and <span class="m-maslow rf-person">Arkadi Maslow</span>, with Hamburg behind it, calls that opportunism dressed up as tactics. <span class="m-radek rf-person">Karl Radek</span> and <span class="m-kolarov rf-person">Vasil Kolarov</span> have come from Moscow to keep the quarrel from becoming a split.

The delegates come from the districts, and each district sends mostly its own majority's people.

{!<div class="rf-district-wrap">!}[+ leipzig_districts +]{!</div>!}

{!<div class="rf-delegates">!}[+ leipzig_delegates +]{!<div class="rf-legend">!}[+ leipzig_legend +]{!</div></div>!}

The motions are the Zentrale's, and it is the Zentrale that decides in what form they go before the hall. The <span class="f-right">Brandler Right</span> will not put its name to what it thinks wrong, and every concession made to Berlin is noted in the Saxon delegations.

- @agenda: Open the congress.

@agenda
new-page: true
on-arrival: {! window.RF.motionIntro(Q, 'leipzig', 'agenda'); !}

= [+ mo_title +]

[+ mo_text +]

- @agenda_keep
- @agenda_report
- @agenda_ruhr

@agenda_keep
title: Hold to the agenda as the Zentrale drew it up.
subtitle: The Ruhr will have its place in the political report, and nowhere else. [+ mo_st_agenda_keep +]
choose-if: mo_ok_agenda_keep = 1
unavailable-subtitle: [+ mo_why_agenda_keep +]
on-arrival: {! window.RF.motionVote(Q, 'leipzig', 'agenda', 'keep'); !}
go-to: agenda_done

@agenda_report
title: Promise the Ruhr a full hour in the political report.
subtitle: Brandler will answer Berlin from the rostrum, on ground of his choosing. [+ mo_st_agenda_report +]
choose-if: mo_ok_agenda_report = 1
unavailable-subtitle: [+ mo_why_agenda_report +]
on-arrival: {! window.RF.motionVote(Q, 'leipzig', 'agenda', 'report'); !}
go-to: agenda_done

@agenda_ruhr
title: Give the occupation a debate of its own, first on the agenda.
subtitle: Berlin gets its debate, and the Zentrale must defend its record before it has made its case. [+ mo_st_agenda_ruhr +]
choose-if: mo_ok_agenda_ruhr = 1
unavailable-subtitle: [+ mo_why_agenda_ruhr +]
on-arrival: {! window.RF.motionVote(Q, 'leipzig', 'agenda', 'ruhr'); !}
go-to: agenda_done

@agenda_done
new-page: true

